#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f279c6af-692d-54f6-9e78-cab02b95d186" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Dilution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "8438bb87-bee8-594a-98a6-4c0117ba64c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21751" :question/problem "A vat initially holds $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of a sugar solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar.\nA sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of sugar per liter is poured into the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ What differential equation describes the amount of sugar $Q,$ in kilograms, in the vat $t$ minutes after the inflow begins?" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =5.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =5.4-\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{60-2t}Q=5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "a4777bc9-c65f-5da2-b962-96b38daa7518" :question/math-academy-id "e-21752" :question/problem "A reactor initially holds $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of dissolved reactant.\nPure solvent is pumped into the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed chemical solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7Q}{150}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{7Q}{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{7}{150} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{7}{150})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{7}{150}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{7t}{150}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{7t}{150}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-7t/150},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-7\\cdot 0/150} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c554a0f7-dfa1-5329-b149-60e64aa1faab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa89a9d-8785-50a9-a517-201f7514136c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21755" :question/problem "A $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$-capacity tank initially holds $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water containing $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\textrm{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 8\\,000 + 100t - 60t = 40(200+t)\\,\\textrm{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{40(200 + t)}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{3}{2(200 + t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{3/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 320,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =320 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{3/2}} & =320 \\\\ A & =320(200)^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{320(200)^{3/2}}{(200+t)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =320{(\\frac{200}{200+t})}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 12\\,000$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 40(200+{t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =320{(\\frac{200}{200+100})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =320{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3/2} \\\\  & ≈174.19\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115373 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f279c6af-692d-54f6-9e78-cab02b95d186" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8044bd9-9c69-5038-8c52-fc8f726d9531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt} - \\frac{8}{150 - t}Q = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113683} {:answer/id #uuid "dd707408-7202-575f-b59c-f13ea66be4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{7}{150 - t}Q = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113684} {:answer/id #uuid "295374a5-dc24-56a6-a0a2-7633dece9fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{8}{150 - t}Q = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113685} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6eb109-acf9-58b5-bb33-fbdff3675f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt} - \\frac{7}{150 - t}Q = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113686} {:answer/id #uuid "7019544e-7b04-559e-aa53-e74900a49d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dQ}{dt} + \\frac{1}{150 - t}Q = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113687}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a41b96bf-c760-5d9e-965d-35966e8ead16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113682}] :question/id #uuid "01e441d3-5537-5224-9166-58a5232542e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335825" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $450L$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $9kg$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $21L/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24L/min$. Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water begins to be added?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c7762cd8-8692-5ad4-968f-3c3e39a3e1f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113688}] :question/id #uuid "64193270-c02d-55f7-b1b5-b71007015ee2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335865" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $120gal$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $20lb$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$.\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t)$, in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added.$Q(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "835ed941-71af-508b-b962-5bfd810b7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "835ed941-71af-508b-b962-5bfd810b7918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{e}^{-t/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374822} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c7259c-bd73-5489-8857-2aa304112761" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc17f053-4154-5b58-a673-a3b7c9595b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-336630" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $260\\,\\text{L}$ of a dye solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye.\nPure water is poured into the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the pure water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye) enters the tank at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of solution in the tank remains $260\\,\\text{L}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $13\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(13\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(13\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13Q}{260}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{20} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{20} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{20})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{20}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/20+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/20},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18{e}^{-t/20}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "587ad7eb-e7ff-5449-a3e7-315421a5c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=20{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374828} {:answer/id #uuid "95d74708-bfdc-53f7-9971-439077d9c125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=2500{(\\frac{10}{100+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374829} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ca561d-2102-5596-b774-af92e2184dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25\\frac{100}{(100+t)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374830} {:answer/id #uuid "28f84566-0022-5a52-aa79-a865a20593a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374831} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0482-ab86-5439-a2a9-8a2e97fe0fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=20{(\\frac{10}{100+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f84566-0022-5a52-aa79-a865a20593a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q(t)=25{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5507560-e644-5b06-9ffc-b163d1f6589b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3c2a5b4-3b5e-50f6-abee-7b39dd34294c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336632" :question/problem "A tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $12t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+18t-12t=(600+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{600+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{2Q}{100+t} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{100+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{100+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{100+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(100+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(100+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ A & =25\\cdot {100}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=25{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115374 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6f8a463-c804-5c12-af05-532e56a257e9" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Dilution Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22446d9b-f3b3-51d7-8eab-f813f126f966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.43\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842} {:answer/id #uuid "fc791120-4af1-5df5-8a8b-8cf249933b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.54\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374843} {:answer/id #uuid "2a979bc1-8055-5284-be10-31b92bc2fb71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.22\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374844} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ab7b77-90c7-560a-a520-39ae98136e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.08\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374845} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6f7cb-44c0-5462-90c0-5e816014480d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.70\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22446d9b-f3b3-51d7-8eab-f813f126f966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.43\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374842} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9b1d72-7dcf-5756-8025-f1f5167127d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7733334-ec35-5062-9a3d-109d06df1cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336674" :question/problem "A wastewater holding basin initially holds $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminants.\nClean water is pumped into the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid leaves the basin at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminants in the basin at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminants enter the basin minus the rate at which contaminants leave the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants enter the basin.\n\nNote that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminants) enters the basin at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminants* enter the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $480\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater; water enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n480+24t-18t=(480+6t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{480+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{3}{80+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{80+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{80+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{80+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(80+t)^{3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $12\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=12,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =12 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(80+0)^{3}} & =12 \\\\ A & =12\\cdot {80}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12{(\\frac{80}{80+t})}^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $10$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=12{(\\frac{80}{90})}^{3}≈8.43\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c554a0f7-dfa1-5329-b149-60e64aa1faab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d90ed3-593b-56eb-b15c-74fb96a264c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374856} {:answer/id #uuid "23a41576-4255-58aa-aff3-24970b0750f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374857} {:answer/id #uuid "162b2841-c921-5383-b411-667946024eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374858} {:answer/id #uuid "a400a2ae-64d3-5df6-8b72-04dc312a8573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374859} {:answer/id #uuid "2c007732-c2d9-5745-a9ef-073e3655a986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a41576-4255-58aa-aff3-24970b0750f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374857} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cb9083-a221-51ed-ad2c-ae6d13e72d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45ce4d33-a7f8-5fd0-bbf2-c53f0966bb82" :question/math-academy-id "q-336727" :question/problem "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity reactor initially holds $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a chemical solution containing $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $7\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,500+90t-60t=30(250+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(250+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{250+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{250+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{250+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{250+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{250+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|250+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(250+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(250+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $98\\,\\text{g}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=98,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =98 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(250+0)^{2}} & =98 \\\\ A & =98(250)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{98(250)^{2}}{(250+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(250+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 250+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =98{(\\frac{250}{250+100})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{250}{350})}^{2} \\\\  & =98{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =50\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c554a0f7-dfa1-5329-b149-60e64aa1faab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb4e1cb-a4d9-5a79-8bad-83176cb4a640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.60\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374863} {:answer/id #uuid "97b47761-bf18-5c2c-807b-5fbd6eecd397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.85\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374864} {:answer/id #uuid "f9316b8e-3e20-5a4c-8d57-cf7e0b5d688e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.46\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374865} {:answer/id #uuid "8dde20d6-6676-5b10-94da-9eee8eb3183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.19\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374866} {:answer/id #uuid "07f0a2a1-f819-5785-94c7-449359e5a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.02\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07f0a2a1-f819-5785-94c7-449359e5a659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.02\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374867} :answer-field/id #uuid "617c7a09-689e-563c-b4f9-daf6d3871ab7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a99a5d4e-3d03-5070-bc7f-206f5695b61b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336822" :question/problem "A $310\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity reactor initially holds $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of a chemical solution containing $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. Pure solvent begins to flow into the reactor at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=190+6t-4t=2(95+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{95+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{190+2t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{190+2t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{95+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(95+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(95+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(95)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(190)^{2}}{(95+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $310\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=310$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =310 \\\\ 190+2{t}_{o} & =310 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{155})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈12.02\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c554a0f7-dfa1-5329-b149-60e64aa1faab" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Amount at an Overflow Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89eecff6-8175-550d-ad43-2b9766bd7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67.49\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374870} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed6a48d-1428-52d9-a522-2bee31663016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.75\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374871} {:answer/id #uuid "17ca700c-b4a3-5002-abdc-7f9a8e703bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.98\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374872} {:answer/id #uuid "51f09e87-40c3-5d6f-9b67-9acbd58b27a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70.10\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374873} {:answer/id #uuid "5118b033-cfac-501e-bbca-b3f1d3ca44b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.42\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ed6a48d-1428-52d9-a522-2bee31663016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.75\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374871} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfba8ca-3fd1-51fd-9b05-5695d3defec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98bdf48f-bd8c-581b-9efb-c84bc03b4bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336826" :question/problem "A $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity vat initially holds $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. Pure water begins to flow into the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed sugar-water flows out at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure water enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,500+100t-80t=20(325+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{325+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{325+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{325+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{325+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|325+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(325+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=300,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =300 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(325+0)^{4}} & =300 \\\\ A & =300(325)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{300(325)^{4}}{(325+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+t})}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. The vat has a capacity of $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 20(325+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 325+{t}_{o} & =475 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =150\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(150) & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+150})}^{4} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{13}{19})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈65.75\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
